    meditation: `Aquinas, in a sentence that has carried weight for eight hundred years, defines justice as a habit. Not a verdict. Not a feeling. Not the rare clean win at the end of a clean case. A habit, perpetua et constans voluntas: the steady, repeated turning of the will toward giving each person what is owed them. He inherits the phrase from Ulpian, the Roman jurist, and Ulpian inherited it from older men still. Eight centuries on this side, sitting in a chair at the end of a long week, you inherit it from Aquinas.

This matters because outcomes lie. A case can come out cleanly when nothing just was done; a case can come out badly when every reasonable hand worked for the right thing. The judge can rule against you on a Tuesday and the world will not have become unjust because of it. The judge can rule for you, and your client may have been wrong all along. To pin your sense of justice to the docket entry is to live by the most unstable indicator on the dashboard. Aquinas refuses you that consolation, and refuses you that despair. He moves the question inward, where it belongs, and asks whether the will is constant.

Constant is the harder word. Perpetual is its twin. Justice is not what you summon for the high-stakes oral argument and let lapse for the routine demand letter. It is what governs the way you answer the paralegal's question at 4:47 PM when your motion deadline is in thirteen minutes. It is the way you describe opposing counsel to your associate when she will never overhear. It is whether you tell the new client at intake that her case is weaker than she hopes, or whether you take the retainer and figure out later how to break it to her. The habit is built, or unbuilt, in those minutes. The big moments only reveal which it was.

Micheas, when the prophet is pressed to say what the Lord requires, refuses to inflate the answer. He does not say to win, to prevail, to defeat the unjust. He says: to do judgment, to love mercy, to walk solicitous with thy God. The Douay's word — solicitous — catches what other translations dilute as humble. The Hebrew underneath carries the sense of watchful attentiveness, the careful step of one who knows the path is narrow and that God is walking with him. Justice in this verse is a verb you do, not a state you arrive at. It pairs with mercy, which is its sister and not its rival, and with a quality of attention to the One who walks beside you. You do not do justice and then love mercy and then walk with God as if these were three errands. They are one motion.

The temptation, when justice becomes habit, is to imagine you have it. Aquinas is careful here. He calls justice a virtue precisely because it is not automatic. The will must be reset to it again, and again, against the drift of self-interest. The settlement on the table is bigger than the case is worth, and the client will sign because he is tired. The young associate you are training has not yet learned to spot the bad-faith email; you can let her sign it, or you can stop and explain. The motion is due, and the easier argument is the dishonest one; the honest one will lose. In each of these the will turns, or it does not.

Notice what Aquinas adds: justice "does not concern the agent in himself, but in relation to another." This is the part the lawyer must keep before his face. The other virtues — temperance, fortitude — order the self. Justice orders the self toward someone else. It is the most outward of the cardinal virtues. You cannot do justice in a room by yourself. There is always a debtor and a creditor, an injured party and the one who must answer, a client and a counterparty and a court and a public. Every act of justice is a small acknowledgment that you are not alone in the world, and that the other person standing there has a claim on you that does not depend on whether you find them sympathetic.

This is why the billable hour, when it runs the show, corrodes the habit. Not because the billable hour is wicked in itself — it is only a unit of measurement — but because measurement, sustained long enough, becomes formation. If the day is structured so that every six minutes must be accounted for and assigned to a matter, the habit of asking what is owed to this person, here, now, gets squeezed by the prior question of what is billable to whom. The pro bono case, which by definition pays nothing, has to fight for its place against the matter that is paying for the lights. The habit of justice, if it is real, will sometimes lose that fight on the calendar and win it in the deeper accounting.

So begin the week here: with a definition you did not invent, and that you do not have to defend. Justice is a habit. The will turns toward what is owed, and turns again tomorrow, and the day after, until the turning is the shape of who you are. You will not feel virtuous. The cases will keep coming out however they come out. But somewhere inside the long arithmetic of your working life, the habit is forming, or it is not. That is the one thing you can attend to today. Walk solicitous. The path is narrow, but you are not alone on it.`,
