    meditation: `If distributive justice is the city dividing what is common, commutative justice is the exchange between two private parties — the buying and selling, the contract, the loan repaid, the damages assessed when one person has injured another. Aquinas calls its mean arithmetical: thing for thing, equal for equal. The seller hands over the bushel of grain, the buyer hands over the price; if the bushel was short, the buyer has been wronged in the precise amount by which it was short, and that amount is what must be restored.

Leviticus, with its just balance and equal weights, is the older form of the same principle. The verse is concrete in a way the Summa is not: it speaks of bushels and sextaries, the actual measuring vessels in the actual marketplace. The temptation to short the buyer was as old as commerce, and the answer was always the same — the balance must be just, the weights equal. The legal mind, three thousand years later, still works mostly in this register. Most of what fills a law office is commutative. The contract that allocates risk between two sophisticated parties. The personal injury suit that asks what was taken from the plaintiff and what must be paid back. The divorce that divides what was joined. The estate that distributes what the dead person owed and owned.

You spend your professional life on the arithmetic mean. The skill of practicing law is largely the skill of seeing what is owed in a given exchange and articulating it precisely enough that a court or a counterparty will recognize it. The medical bills are real numbers. The lost wages are real numbers. The diminished value of the property is a real number, even when the appraisers disagree. Commutative justice is comfortable for the lawyer because it is the kind of justice the law was built to enforce; the law's machinery is tuned to it. You do not need a theology of the common good to handle a breach-of-contract claim. You need to read the contract, calculate the damages, and bring the case.

And yet. The clean arithmetic of commutation hides distortions everywhere. The settlement that closes the file for sixty cents on the dollar because the plaintiff cannot afford to wait. The arbitration clause buried in the terms of service that channels disputes into a forum where the corporate defendant always wins. The pre-litigation demand letter that asks for three times the case's value because everybody knows you have to start high. The discovery dispute that runs the meter on the other side until they capitulate. Each of these is a corruption of the arithmetic. The thing-for-thing equality is replaced by a power-for-thing equality, where what you can extract depends on what you can outlast.

Leviticus knows this danger and addresses it head-on. The balance must be just. The reason given is striking: "I am the Lord your God, that brought you out of the land of Egypt." The grounding is not commercial efficiency. It is the memory of slavery. A people who have been on the receiving end of unjust weights — who built the storehouses of Pharaoh and were given short measure in return — are commanded never to do this to anyone else. The just balance is a covenantal balance. To weigh dishonestly is to betray the God who weighed in your favor when no one else would.

The Christian lawyer carries a similar memory, though it runs through Calvary rather than the Red Sea. We were the debtor who could not pay. The accounts were settled by another. To then turn around and squeeze the actual debtor for everything the legal system permits is a kind of forgetting that the parable of the unmerciful servant addresses with some severity. The point is not that commutative justice should be replaced by mercy in every case. Sometimes the bushel really was short, and the seller should pay back the difference, and the lawyer's job is to see that this happens. The point is that the arithmetic itself must not be cooked, and the lawyer who cooks it for advantage is on the wrong side of an older account.

The contract is the central scene of commutative justice. When you draft one, you are doing a small act of legislation between two parties: you are setting the weights and measures by which they will deal with each other. The clauses you include and the clauses you omit will, months or years from now, determine who gets what when something goes wrong. The boilerplate is not neutral. The indemnification provision shifts risk; the limitation of liability caps exposure; the venue clause picks a courtroom; the integration clause forecloses arguments that might otherwise have been made. Each of these is a weight on the balance. Some of them are bargained-for, knowingly accepted; some are slipped past a counterparty who is not paying close attention. The latter is the cousin of the short bushel.

The negotiation is the other central scene. The exchange of demands, the back and forth, the eventual landing place — all of this is the arithmetic working itself out in slow motion. The skill is in seeing accurately what the case is worth, what the contract is worth, what the deal is worth, and pressing for that and not more. To press for more, when you have the leverage to extract it, is to take what is not yours. The leverage may be your client's deep pockets, the counterparty's exhaustion, the asymmetric information, the threat of years of further litigation. Commutative justice asks you to set these aside and look at the underlying transaction. What was given? What is owed? What is the true equal?

Almost no client will thank you for this. The client wants the maximum they can get, and the partner wants the result that pads the recovery, and the contingency fee is a percentage of whatever you can wring out. The pressures are real, and they are not going away. But the just balance is older than these pressures, and it will outlast them. Today, somewhere in a draft on your screen, the balance can be set true or set false. Set it true.`,
