    meditation: `Aristotle, in the eighth book of the Ethics, makes a strange and beautiful claim. Where there is friendship there is no need of justice. He does not mean that friends can cheat one another. He means that between true friends the question of what is owed does not have to be raised, because each is already inclined to give the other more than is owed. Justice is the floor; friendship walks well above it. And then Aristotle says the harder thing: when men are just they still need friendship. Justice without friendship is cold. Friendship without justice is unstable. The two complete each other.

Aquinas takes the thought into the Summa, and into the lives of those who would be Christian friends to one another. The truest form of justice, he says — quoting back through Aristotle — has a friendly quality. The just person, in the deepest sense, is not the bookkeeper who keeps accounts immaculate. The just person is the one for whom the rendering of what is owed has become so natural that it shades into the freely given. Friendship adds to justice. The two are not opposed; they are a continuum.

This matters for the lawyer in two directions, and both of them are uncomfortable.

The first direction is inward, toward the people you actually work with. The partners and associates and paralegals and assistants and the opposing counsel you see across the table every Tuesday. The bar of a city, especially a smaller bar, is a community. You will run into these people for thirty years. The question Aquinas presses is whether justice between you has any friendly quality at all, or whether it has hardened into a defensive minimum — the bare professional courtesy that lets you function without trusting one another. The latter is sustainable, in the short run. It is also corrosive. The lawyer who treats every opposing counsel as a potential adversary in a disciplinary complaint, who copies every email to three other people for paper, who answers every reasonable request with a citation to the rules — this lawyer has justice, perhaps, but justice of the most arid kind. Aristotle would say it is not yet the truest form.

The friendly quality of justice is not naivete. You can be friendly and still hold the line. You can return the call promptly, grant the extension when nothing is lost by granting it, take the call at the end of a long day from the associate at the other firm who is trying to figure something out, and still represent your client with everything you have when the substantive issue is joined. Indeed the willingness to do these things is part of what makes you trustworthy when the substantive issue is joined. The bar that has lost its friendly quality has lost something important about its capacity to do justice.

The second direction is outward, toward the client. The client is not your friend in Aristotle's sense — there is a fee, a structure, an asymmetry that friendship cannot survive — but the friendly quality of justice should still color the relationship. The client is paying you to render what is owed: candid advice, competent representation, attention to the matter. But friendship would give over and above. Friendship would notice that the client is exhausted by the litigation and recommend the settlement that would let her sleep. Friendship would call after the case is over to see how she is doing. Friendship would, in some small way, treat the case not as a billable matter but as a moment in another person's life that you were privileged to walk through.

The pro bono client, especially, sits in this space. Without the fee, the structure of professional distance collapses. The client may have nothing to give you except the relationship itself. The temptation, when there is no money in it, is to give less attention rather than more — to slot the case into the spare hours. Friendship would reverse the calculation. The Ecclesiasticus passage is talking about something deeper than collegiality: a faithful friend is the medicine of life. The lawyer who shows up faithfully for a pro bono client over the months or years of a hard case is not doing charity. He is being, for a little while, a faithful friend, which the scripture says is a strong defense and a treasure that cannot be weighed.

There is a third direction, which is the friendship of lawyers with each other. The profession is hard, and getting harder. The hours, the pressure, the moral weight of standing for other people's interests, the slow erosion of the boundary between work and the rest of life — these grind on every advocate. The young lawyer who does not find friends inside the profession will burn out, or will harden, or will leave. The friendships you form with colleagues who share the work are not adjacent to your vocation; they are part of how the vocation is sustained. Aquinas would say the friendship is itself a kind of justice, because each of you is giving the other something owed to a fellow human walking the same difficult path.

The Ecclesiasticus author closes with a small theological turn. They that fear the Lord shall find a faithful friend. He that feareth God shall likewise have good friendship: because according to him shall his friend be. The friendships you build are shaped by what you bring to them. If you bring the fear of the Lord — meaning the habit of reverent attention to what is owed — you will draw to yourself people who do the same. The bar you complain about is partly the bar you are making. The colleagues you wish were more honorable are partly the colleagues you are forming by what you tolerate and what you praise.

Set the bar where Aristotle set it. Justice as the floor, friendship as the room above the floor, and the willingness to live in the room rather than in the floorboards.`,
