    meditation: `Augustine knew that the loudest courtroom is the one inside the skull. Long before he was a bishop he had been a rhetorician — paid, in his own description, to make the worse case appear the better — and he understood from the inside how a man can win an argument and still lose the case before his own conscience. The Confessions, taken as a whole, is the record of an interior trial conducted across the years. The witness, the accused, the prosecutor, and the judge are all the same man, and the only true verdict is the one God renders. The rest is shadow play.

Paul, in Romans 2, sketches the same court in a single sentence. The Gentiles, who do not have the Mosaic law, are nevertheless a law to themselves: the work of the law is written on their hearts, and their conscience bears witness, and their thoughts go back and forth between themselves accusing or also defending. The picture is forensic. There is a charge. There is a defense. There is a verdict in the heart. Paul will go on, in chapter 14, to say that whatever is not of faith is sin, and his ethics finally rest on the integrity of this interior court rather than on the external rule.

For the lawyer this image cuts twice. First, it presses the question of self-knowledge. You are trained to construct arguments. You are trained, by long practice, to find the angle that recasts a difficult fact in a favorable light. You can do this for clients all day, and at five o'clock you can do it for yourself. The settlement you took for sixty cents on the dollar becomes, in the retelling, a brilliant risk-managed outcome. The associate you under-credited becomes, in the partner meeting, a junior who was not quite ready. The hours you padded — by ten percent, by fifteen, only on the matter that everyone pads — become, in the time entry, a fair approximation of value delivered. None of this is told to anyone. All of it is told to yourself.

Augustine watches this happen and is suspicious of the audience problem. What have I to do with men, that they should hear my confessions, as if they could heal all my infirmities? The lawyer's instinct is to bring every dispute before some external tribunal — a judge, a partner, an ethics committee, a senior friend. These are useful, often necessary. But Augustine is pointing at a deeper court: the one in which God hears not what you say about yourself but what you actually are. The Confessions are addressed to God, in the second person, throughout. Augustine is not telling Carthage about Augustine; he is telling Augustine about Augustine, in the presence of God, who already knows.

The interior court has its own corruptions. The conscience can be silenced by repetition: do something often enough and the inner voice tires of objecting. The conscience can be drowned by volume: keep the schedule full enough and there is no quiet hour in which the verdict could be heard. The conscience can be bribed by self-image: tell yourself often enough that you are a good lawyer, a good person, a faithful Christian, and the disconfirming evidence gets ruled inadmissible. Augustine knew all of these. So did Paul. The accusing and defending thoughts go back and forth, and the verdict that emerges may have very little to do with what actually happened.

The remedy is not introspection in the modern sense — endless self-examination tends to feed the very ego it claims to chasten. The remedy is what Augustine is doing in the Confessions: bringing oneself before the One who already knows, and letting His knowing be the standard. This is what makes confession in the Christian sense different from therapy or self-help. The point is not to feel better about yourself. The point is to be seen truly, by the One whose seeing is also healing. The interior court, when it is conducted in the presence of God, ceases to be a place where you negotiate with yourself and becomes a place where you are finally honest.

For the lawyer the practical shape of this is small and daily. At the end of the day, before the report to the spouse or the dinner with the family or the second wind of evening work, a few minutes in which the day is brought before God without spin. Not a recitation of accomplishments. Not a list of grievances. A simple looking back: where did I render what was owed today, and where did I not? Where did I tell the truth about a case, to my client or to myself, and where did I dress it up? Where did I use my skill for someone, and where did I use it on someone? The questions are not designed to produce shame. They are designed to produce the kind of accurate self-knowledge that prevents the slow drift into the lawyer you did not intend to become.

Augustine's lifelong project was to live coram Deo — before God's face — rather than coram hominibus, before men's. The lawyer's whole training pulls in the opposite direction. You perform before judges, before juries, before clients, before partners, before the firm website. The interior court, conducted in the presence of God, is the only counterweight to this constant performance. It is the place where, finally, you do not have to make a case. You are simply known. And being known, and loved within the knowing, is what makes it possible to walk back out tomorrow and try again to render to each what is theirs — including yourself.

The verse from Romans ends with a phrase the lawyer should keep close: "in the day when God shall judge the secrets of men by Jesus Christ." The secret part. The part no opposing counsel saw, no ethics opinion captured, no time entry recorded. That is the part that finally matters. Cultivate the interior court now, gently and habitually, so that on that day there is not too much that comes as a surprise.`,
