    meditation: `Augustine's sentence has outlived empires. Remota itaque iustitia, quid sunt regna nisi magna latrocinia? Justice being taken away, what are kingdoms but great robberies? It is the most quoted line of the City of God and probably the most uncomfortable, because Augustine is not making a rhetorical point. He is making a structural one. The difference between a band of robbers and a kingdom is scale and impunity, not nature. A robber takes by force what is not his and divides the spoils by an agreed law. A kingdom does the same on a larger map, with better uniforms. Without justice — without the rendering of suum cuique — the only distinguishing feature is the size of the fleet.

The pirate's answer to Alexander is the line that ought to hang in every courthouse. Alexander asks the pirate by what right he keeps hostile possession of the sea, and the pirate answers: by the same right by which you keep hostile possession of the earth. You do it with a great fleet and are styled emperor. I do it with a small ship and am called a robber. The pirate's effrontery is also his philosophy. Power dressed as law is still power. Conquest blessed by jurists is still conquest. The lawyer, who is among the jurists, has a particular reason to listen to the pirate.

Isaias makes the point in the prophetic register. Woe to them that make wicked laws, and when they write, write injustice. The Hebrew prophets understood that injustice could be perfectly legal. The wicked law is written, signed, and enforced. The judges sit, the procedures are followed, the rolls are kept, and the widow's house is still consumed and the orphan's portion is still robbed. The wickedness is not in the violation of the law; it is in the law itself, and in the system that produces such laws and the officers who implement them. The prophet's woe is directed at those who write injustice, which is to say, at the legislators and the lawyers who give such writing its form.

This is the structural critique Augustine and Isaias jointly press, and the lawyer cannot dodge it. The legal system you serve is not transparent to justice. It can be — sometimes it is — a great robbery dressed in robes. Slave codes were laws. Jim Crow was law. The expropriations of indigenous peoples were carried out by deed and statute. The discriminatory zoning ordinance, the predatory consumer contract enforced by courts, the asset forfeiture regime that takes from the poor what they cannot afford to fight to recover — all of these are law. The lawyer who treats law as automatically just because it is law has missed everything Augustine and the prophets are saying. The question is always whether the law is the kingdom or the robbery.

This is not a counsel of nihilism. Augustine is not telling you to abandon the law. The City of God is, among other things, a defense of legitimate political authority and a counsel of patience with the imperfect arrangements of the earthly city. Aquinas, eight centuries later, builds the whole edifice of just-law theory on Augustinian foundations: a law that is not directed to the common good, or that exceeds the lawmaker's authority, or that violates the divine law, lacks the character of law and does not bind in conscience as law does. But Aquinas can build that edifice only because Augustine first refused to baptize the kingdom as such. The kingdom is a kingdom only when justice is in it. When justice is taken away, the throne is no different from the captain's perch on the pirate ship.

For the working lawyer this lands in three places.

First, in the cases you take. Not every client is the widow whose cause Isaias is defending. Some of your clients are, structurally, closer to the pirate. The corporate defendant whose pollution is permitted by an industry-captured regulator. The landlord whose evictions are technically lawful and substantively rapacious. The lender whose contract was drafted to extract every fee a court will tolerate. You may have professional reasons to take such matters; not every defendant is wrong; the adversary system depends on competent representation across the board. But you cannot pretend the question does not exist. You are operating, sometimes, inside a legal kingdom that is partly a robbery, and your skill is being lent to one side of the operation.

Second, in the laws themselves. The lawyer is uniquely placed to see where the writing is unjust. You read the statutes; you litigate them; you know which provisions exist to protect rights and which exist to defeat them. The bar's traditional role in law reform — the report of the standing committee, the amicus brief, the testimony before the legislature, the unglamorous work on the uniform laws — is one of the ways the profession answers Isaias. The lawyer who does no law reform of any kind, ever, has narrowed the office to mere advocacy and left the wider question of what is being advocated to other hands.

Third, in the disposition. Augustine's sentence is corrosive to triumphalism. It will not let you celebrate the legal system as such, because you know what it has been and what it can be. Equally, it will not let you despair of it, because the difference between the kingdom and the robbery is exactly the presence or absence of justice, and justice is something you can put back in, one act at a time, one case at a time. The pirate's complaint to Alexander is also a hidden hope. If the only difference is scale and impunity, then the addition of justice can convert the robbery into a kingdom, and the subtraction of justice can convert the kingdom into a robbery. The lawyer's daily work is on the hinge of that conversion.

You will not save the kingdom. Augustine does not ask you to. But you can refuse to be one of those who write injustice, and you can use the small portion of the writing that is in your hands to write the other thing. The widow whose cause is yours this week is the test of whether the system, on her one case, will be the kingdom or the robbery. The pirate is watching from the deck. So is the King.`,
